  "description": "Construct an ensemble of decision trees with feature subsampling and aggregate individual predictions via majority voting.\n\nRandom forests reduce variance without increasing bias by averaging decorrelated decision trees.\n\n### Mathematical Formulation\n$$\n\\hat{y} = \\operatorname{mode}\\left(T_1(x), T_2(x), \\dots, T_B(x)\\right)\n$$\n\n### Step-by-Step Implementation\n1. Train B tree estimators on feature subsets and decision boundaries\n2. Generate test predictions for each individual tree\n3. Combine predictions through majority voting across the ensemble\n\n### Returns\nList of integer class predictions for the test samples.",
  "theory": "## Understanding the Theory: Random Forest from Scratch\n\n### Core Intuition: Why Do We Need This?\nIn computational mathematics and machine learning, robust algorithmic routines ensure calculations are both mathematically sound and computationally efficient. **Random Forest from Scratch** implements a core computational building block.\n\nRandom forests reduce variance without increasing bias by averaging decorrelated decision trees. In this problem, the goal is to: construct an ensemble of decision trees with feature subsampling and aggregate individual predictions via majority voting.\n\n### Mathematical Principles & Formulation\nThe core governing equation for this operation is:\n\n$$\n\\hat{y} = \\operatorname{mode}\\left(T_1(x), T_2(x), \\dots, T_B(x)\\right)\n$$\n\nEvery term in this formulation plays a precise mathematical role in ensuring dimensional consistency, numerical stability, and correct algorithmic behavior.\n\n### Step-by-Step Computational Workflow\n1. **Phase 1**: Train B tree estimators on feature subsets and decision boundaries\n2. **Phase 2**: Generate test predictions for each individual tree\n3. **Phase 3**: Combine predictions through majority voting across the ensemble\n\n### Practical Machine Learning Applications & Nuances\n* **Algorithmic Complexity**: Designing routines with optimal time and space complexity ensures they scale gracefully to large datasets.\n* **Numerical Accuracy**: Safeguarding calculations against floating-point underflow or division by zero ensures consistent results.\n* **Vectorization**: Implementing operations with contiguous array buffers avoids interpreter overhead.",
