  "starterCode": "import numpy as np\n\ndef ml_random_forest(X_train: list, y_train: list, X_test: list, n_trees: int = 5) -> list:\n    pass",
  "solution": "### 💡 Intuition & Approach\nThe implementation computes **Random Forest from Scratch** by following direct mathematical principles, leveraging vectorized operations to ensure both correctness and optimal runtime efficiency.\n\n### ⚡ Python Reference Implementation\n```python\nimport numpy as np\n\ndef ml_random_forest(X_train: list, y_train: list, X_test: list, n_trees: int = 5) -> list:\n    X, y, Xt = np.array(X_train, dtype=np.float64), np.array(y_train, dtype=np.int64), np.array(X_test, dtype=np.float64)\n    n_feats = X.shape[1]\n    preds = []\n    for i in range(int(n_trees)):\n        f = i % n_feats\n        th = np.median(X[:, f])\n        lm, rm = X[:, f] <= th, X[:, f] > th\n        yl, yr = y[lm], y[rm]\n        pl = int(np.bincount(yl).argmax()) if len(yl) > 0 else 0\n        pr = int(np.bincount(yr).argmax()) if len(yr) > 0 else 0\n        preds.append(np.where(Xt[:, f] <= th, pl, pr))\n    all_p = np.array(preds)\n    return [int(np.bincount(all_p[:, j]).argmax()) for j in range(len(Xt))]\n```\n\n### ⏱️ Complexity Analysis\n- **Time Complexity**: $\\mathcal{O}(N)$ where $N$ represents the size of the primary input.\n- **Space Complexity**: $\\mathcal{O}(N)$ auxiliary memory for computing and storing output representations.",
